WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:06.640
At the end of March this year, NASA astronaut
Scott Kelly and Russian Cosmonaut Mikhail

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.840
Kornienko launched into space to start a year-long
mission at the International Space Station

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.630
And they did this to test the effects of long-term
space travel on the human body.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:19.199
This’ll be the longest trip ever taken by
a NASA astronaut.

00:00:19.199 --> 00:00:21.270
Most trips usually last between 4-6 months.

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:25.429
There are a bunch of issues with spending
time in space we already know about — muscles

00:00:25.429 --> 00:00:29.130
waste away, there’s bone loss, and circulatory
system troubles.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:34.820
“It’s amazing how your body adapts to
this environment and how it remembers actually

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:35.820
being here”

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:41.110
Space programs have ways of preparing astronauts
for space itself: there’s underwater practice,

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:44.510
there’s parachute training, there’s even
this airplane called the “vomit comet”

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:46.100
that simulates zero-G.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.550
But staying healthy in space is a bit different.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.080
Exercise is a big issue.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.860
On Earth, our muscles and bones stay strong
because we have to constantly fight against

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:55.860
gravity.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:59.610
Without gravity, they get weak, and that can
prevent astronauts from doing important physical

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:01.340
tasks on the job.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:05.119
To prevent this, they’re required to do
up to two and a half hours of exercise per

00:01:05.119 --> 00:01:10.150
day, strapped into cycling machines, treadmills,
and resistance lifting machines.

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:14.240
During long periods in space, astronauts also
develop a weakened immune system.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.360
It doesn’t adapt the way it does back on
earth, which leaves astronauts prone to infection

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.920
due to immune system dysregulation.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.670
And this his is a pretty recent discovery.

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:26.230
Scientists aren’t exactly sure what causes
this to happen — it could be any combination

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:31.030
of lack of sleep, zero gravity, radiation,
and isolation.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:36.030
Even beyond the physical complications, the
psychological aspect is significant, too.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:39.229
Astronauts are in tight, closed conditions
with other people, and their days are not

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:40.950
defined by natural light.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:44.940
The Space Station orbits the Earth every 90
minutes, which comes out to about 16 times

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.020
per day.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:49.811
Even just getting a good night’s rest can
be difficult: over 75 percent of astronauts

00:01:49.811 --> 00:01:52.190
report using sleeping pills regularly.

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:56.670
Scott Kelly’s twin brother Mark is a retired
astronaut, and he’s serving as a live subject

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.369
of comparison for the effects of spending
time in space.

00:01:59.369 --> 00:02:04.270
And the more we understand about this, the
closer we get to longer and bigger projects

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:05.340
in the final frontier.

